{
 "title": "In 24 held-out OpenNeuro studies, task runs had a 20% shorter cortical BOLD timescale than rest runs after evoked-response removal, with weaker slow fluctuations",
 "abstract": "In fMRI task runs, the ongoing BOLD signal of the cortex lost its memory faster than in rest runs of the same people, and the responses that the task stimuli evoke did not explain most of this. This matters because task-rest differences in signal dynamics are often read as evoked activity or as global-signal change, and no study had separated these parts across many tasks and scanners. We used ReadyNeuro, minimally preprocessed fMRI from OpenNeuro, and compared task and rest runs of each person at the same repetition time (TR), cut to the same length. The intrinsic timescale is the sum of the autocorrelation of a region signal over its first positive lobe, in seconds, averaged over 200 cortical regions. To remove evoked responses, we regressed the event design out of each task run and the same design out of the paired rest run (a sham), so both runs lost the same signal band. Studies were split at random into discovery and confirmation sets; tests were written down before confirmation data were downloaded. In 24 confirmation studies (133 people), the timescale was 0.48 s shorter in task runs after evoked-response removal (95% CI 0.33 to 0.63; rest 2.50 s; Cohen d -1.33; 88% of studies), and 0.42 s shorter after the global signal was also removed (d -1.78; 96% of studies). The median task/rest ratio was 0.80. FIR models, white-matter and CSF regressors and motion-spike regressors did not remove the change, and a bias simulation showed biases much smaller than the effect. A post-hoc analysis showed that power below 0.05 Hz fell by about a quarter, while power at 0.08-0.20 Hz did not change significantly. Before global-signal regression, CSF and white matter changed about as much as cortex; after it, cortex changed 0.28 s more than CSF, about 14% of the rest value. In raw data, block designs hid the change. A predicted link to the regional rest timescale did not replicate. Limitations: rest is not a neutral baseline (eye state and drowsiness are unknown), no physiological recordings, and events files for 24 of 39 confirmation studies.",
